{
  "raw": {
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/Campus-Safety-Officer_R0007395": {
      "title": "Campus Safety Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-16\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY Campus Safety Officers are responsible for helping ensure the safety and security of Willamette University's students, employees, visitors, and property during their assigned shifts. They deal with all violations of law and campus policy in an appropriate and equitable manner and positively engage with diverse individuals and groups in support of an inclusive environment. The Campus Safety Officer position reports to the Associate Director of Campus Safety. This position is responsible for responding to situations utilizing a set of well-defined policies, procedures, and expectations. Must be able to perform duties with independence. Provides direction and supervision to work-study students but has no formal responsibility for supervision. Public relations are an important part of this position. Requires strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills to exchange information with individuals both inside and outside the University. Requires an understanding of a culturally diverse community and the need to interact in a manner that fosters positive relationships in respectful, equitable, and inclusive ways.   Duties and Responsibilities Campus Safety Officers are responsible for helping ensure the safety and security of Willamette University's students, employees, visitors, and property during their assigned shifts. Patrol university grounds and facilities to ensure a safe and secure environment, including on foot and in vehicles, patrol campus facilities, parking lots, grounds, and the interior of all buildings on campus. Monitor all activity and confront any suspicious behavior on Campus. Escort visitors and intruders off campus if required. Issue citations and warnings to violators. Interview individuals involved in incidents, gather information and write reports on crimes, violence, vandalism, fires, and other illegal activities on campus. Give accounts of people involved, who are affected, and what measures were taken to minimize or eliminate the incidents. Respond quickly to all calls to Campus Safety for assistance and contact local Police, Fire, or Medical personnel when necessary. Provides medical care at the basic first aid/CPR/AED level as provided by the American Red Cross or its equivalent. Coordinates with medical responders when a higher level of care is required. Responds to notifications from the security and fire alarms across campus that are monitored by campus Safety. Contribute to the support and implementation of the University Strategic Plan. Adhere to University policies and procedures. Updates daily activity logs as needed and in a timely manner; completes written incident and investigative reports in a timely, clear, and well-written manner. Perform other duties as assigned.   Minimum Qualifications Must meet minimum qualifications for certification standards set by the Department of Public Safety Standards and Training (DPSST). DPSST requires candidates to be a minimum of 18 years of age to obtain an Unarmed Private Security certification. High school diploma or GED Minimum of two years experience in a security position or two years of college-level law enforcement or criminal justice courses And/ Or any combination of education and experience that provides the required knowledge, skills, and abilities to perform the position's essential functions. Ability to operate a computer, with demonstrated skills in Outlook, Word, Excel, and other security-related programs.  Valid driver’s license within 60 days of hire. CPR/AED Certification or obtain within 90 days of employment. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills are necessary, as well as the ability to actively communicate with various constituents, including faculty, staff, administration, and the general public. Demonstrated ability to efficiently and effectively solve problems and initiate and implement projects independently. Ability to multi-task with accuracy and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Experience working with diverse populations. Preferred Qualifications Customer service experience. Experience working in a security-related field. Experience working at a college or university. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Typical schedule requires 40 hour per work with possible required overtime as needed Requires standing for long periods of time and taking many stairs/runs as part of your regular patrol duties.  Requires to kneel/crawl, twist, lift, carry, push, and pull objects that weigh up to 50 pounds. Salary: $22.44/ hour   You will need to upload the following document as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Current Resume   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received until the position is filled.   Incomplete applications will not be considered.  BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/Maintenance-Repair-Worker-II---HVAC_R0007387": {
      "title": "Maintenance Repair Worker II / HVAC",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY The Facilities Department at Willamette University is responsible for the daily operations, maintenance and upkeep of approximately 1.4 million square feet of academic, residential, administrative and off campus properties situated on approximately 60 acres with all the associated utilities and infrastructure. Under general direction, the Maintenance Repair Worker II performs skilled journey level work in more than one trade or craft. Incumbents typically specialize in one trade or craft but perform journey-level and semi-skilled work in a variety of disciplines. Maintenance Repair Workers perform construction, maintenance, repair and modification of buildings, facilities, mechanical equipment, machinery and department or Facilities specific apparatus. Maintenance Repair Workers will utilize a working knowledge of several related skill fields such as plumbing, electrical, HVACR, welding, carpentry, and machinist work in their daily duties. This is the senior/specialist worker level of the Maintenance Repair Worker series. RESPONSIBILITIES Practices and encourages Facilities Management workplace expectations. Inspect, adjust, maintain and repair facilities operating systems, such as HVAC systems, kitchen equipment and pumps/pumping systems. Assists with pneumatic controls, boilers, direct digital controls for boilers and energy management systems. Responds to emergency breakdowns and repairs or replaces needed parts to resume operation. May perform skilled inspections and repairs of specialized systems such as backflow devices, swimming pools and fountains or other systems that require a special license or certification. May perform welding and brazing operations and metal fabrication as directed. Performs preventative maintenance tasks such as changing filters, cleaning and lubrication of operating equipment, cleaning coils, belt changing and alignment, sheaves changes and bearing replacements. Assists in the coordination of the preventive maintenance program through scheduled inspections, equipment service, and maintaining records of repairs and maintenance performed. Accompanies and assists ITM contractors and inspectors. Maintains an inventory of critical spare parts, supplies, and equipment. May coordinate the work of semiskilled assistants or other helpers such as trades/maintenance workers, laborers or students. Operates and makes repairs to power tools related to the trade. Repairs and maintains plumbing as directed. Performs minor roof patching and repair. Fills in on weekends and holidays when required. Subject to after hour’s callback for emergencies. Assists other team members as needed. Performs other maintenance related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Any combination of education, experience and training that provides the required knowledge, skills and abilities to perform the duties of this position. High school diploma or GED desired. Minimum of four years’ experience as a Maintenance Repair Worker involving skilled work related to the maintenance and repair of buildings and related equipment required OR completion of an approved Mechanical Maintenance Apprenticeship Program. Basic computer skills such as typing, email and the ability to fill out and complete time sheets and service request forms online required. Possession of a valid Oregon driver’s license and a satisfactory driving record or the ability to obtain one within 60 days of date of hire. Must have the ability to effectively interact with a variety of people in a manner that emphasizes strong customer service and portrays the University’s programs, personnel and philosophies in a positive manner. Must have the ability to work independently as well as establish and maintain harmonious relations with other staff, faculty and students. Organize and update computerized logs. Routinely and safely lifts 50 pounds. Work while walking, stooping, bending over and/or standing for prolonged periods of time and while exposed to dirt, dust, infectious waste, obnoxious odors, and inclement weather. Estimate time and materials. Climb ladders and work in confined spaces. Communicate both orally and in written English. Have the knowledge and training in the safety hazards and precautions associate with this position and other building maintenance functions. Must have knowledge of and be able to use the appropriate PPE (Personal protective equipment) as needed. Typical Work Schedule: Monday through Friday, 7:30 am to 4:00 pm Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Please review the Qualifications section above for more detailed information regarding physical demands and working conditions. Salary: $25.07/hour You will need to upload the following two documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing qualifications Current resume Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. ​Incomplete applications will not be considered. BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Portland-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Sculpture-and-3D-Practices_R0007385": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Sculpture and 3D Practices",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION OVERVIEW College Background Pacific Northwest College of Art is the oldest professional art & design college in Oregon. It began as the Portland Art Museum school in 1909 and operated independently until its merger with Willamette University in 2021. PNCA is now the second largest college within the University. Willamette University provides rigorous education in the liberal arts, arts & design, law and management through its undergraduate and graduate programs and lifelong learning programs. PNCA has approximately 550 students in 10 undergraduate and 5 graduate programs. PNCA sparks curiosity and sharpens skills so students can build resilient creative careers anchored in innovation, justice and civic imagination. In addition to our academic programs we serve as a cultural “front porch” bringing artists, designers, scholars and community partners together through the Center for Contemporary Art + Culture where we showcase lectures, talks, exhibits and events that connect Portlanders to art, design and creative exploration.   Campus Community PNCA’s campus model is centered on a framework of wellness. We believe artists, designers, writers, scholars must be connected, healthy and collaborative in order to thrive. Our Academic Wellness, Student Affairs, Office of International Education, Maker Spaces and Career Design Center weave together with our teaching faculty to support the whole student, fueling a campus that sparks fierce collaborators, bold makers, and citizen artist/designers.   Faculty at PNCA The primary professional responsibilities of instructional faculty members are: teaching, research, scholarship and/or creative activity, and service to the College and larger University, profession and to the community. These responsibilities generally include: developing and enhancing curriculum, advising students, participation in campus and University-wide committees, maintaining office hours, working collaboratively and productively with colleagues, building external partnerships and industry relationships, and maintaining a vibrant creative practice and engagement with faculty and university governance. PNCA faculty are engaged and active in the classroom, community and in governance.   PNCA’s commitment to Inclusion We strive to create a safe and supportive environment that is enriched by variety in voices, work, and perspectives. We know that we are a better community of learners and educators when we have space for a multiplicity of identities, worldviews and experiences. Thus, broad inclusion at PNCA is crucial to our intellectual and aesthetic inquiry. PNCA is a laboratory for critical reflection and dialogue that nurtures better, more informed, more critically engaged citizens while it educates emerging artists and designers.   Position Summary  Pacific Northwest College of Art at Willamette University (PNCA) is seeking an energetic, inclusive, and committed educator. This faculty member will teach courses, provide leadership in the undergraduate Sculpture department, and contribute to the ongoing development of the undergraduate Foundation and related programs at the college.  RESPONSIBILITIES Teach five courses annually with a focus on Sculpture, Foundation, and related disciplines. Mentor students and coordinate departmental visiting artists and studio visits. Maintain daily presence in 3D shops and labs, oversee the 3D Shops and core staff. Under the direction of the Dean & Sr. Associate Dean, contribute to a 3D facilities program and facilities improvement plan. Lead departmental scheduling, curricular development as necessary. Convene 3D faculty and support professional development and mentorship of part-time faculty, staff and student workers. Participate in admissions recruitment, open houses, portfolio reviews, and industry events as requested by the Dean and Sr. Associate Dean. Under the direction of the Assessment Coordinator, participate in annual assessment planning. Participate in required faculty professional development, orientation and onboarding. Maintain up to date syllabi, aligned with college learning outcomes and minimum standards for Canvas, learning management system. Participate in holistic student care and case management processes. Keep up to date with professional and industry best practices and maintain an external and active creative research practice.  Participate in faculty governance and service to the college. Foster an inclusive, equitable and diverse learning environment.  QUALIFICATIONS Minimum MFA or equivalent professional experience with at least 5 years of relevant higher education or formal community based teaching experience.  Evidence of an active professional practice within the field of sculpture, craft/object based design, or product design. Evidence of experience managing and teaching design thinking, design strategy and best practices. Demonstrated professional and safety qualifications with at least three of the following areas: ceramics, metal, wood, textiles, glass mold/casting, technique and fabrication. Demonstrated proficiency in computer-aided 3D design tools and programs. Up to date safety and materials handling training and certifications. Demonstrated experience in an inclusive teaching environment. Experience managing fabrication shops or facilities and supervising staff or volunteers within lab/shop context. Preferred MFA, or equivalent terminal degree, in, art/design or closely-related field from an accredited institution. Evidence of successful collegiate teaching or rigorous community-based teaching experience in craft design, sculpture or other 3D fabrication. Professional or applied experience in managing a studio, freelance or small business practice related to designed objects, product design, sculpture or sculptural installation. Experience with programs including Rhino, Autodesk Fusion 360, Sketchup, and more Professional and/or teaching applied studio and career skills such as project management and development, curatorial practice, venture management or exhibition installation. Demonstrated skills in both digital and traditional mediums.   Evidence of advancements in their own field of professional practice. Demonstrated professional networks in associated fields of practice.  The successful candidate will be committed to the academic success of all of our students and to an environment that acknowledges, encourages, and celebrates differences. To this end, the successful candidate will work effectively, respectfully, and collaboratively in diverse, multicultural, and inclusive settings. In addition, the successful candidate will be ready to join faculty, staff, students, and administrators in our university’s shared commitment to the principles of engagement, service, and the public good.  Salary: Starting at $62,424 annually To apply, please note that Workday limits material document submissions. We ask that candidates follow instructions to ensure effective material review. You will need to upload the following documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume.   Cover letter highlighting your interest and relevant teaching experience (PDF) Professional resume/CV including hyperlinks to relevant websites and/or professional social media (PDF) Portfolio showcasing 20 pieces of relevant creative work including descriptions, dates, and media. (Single PDF containing all 20 images) A statement of inclusive teaching and at least 5 examples of selected student work, projects or sample assignments with equivalent course information. (Single PDF containing all materials) Application review begins January 11, 2027.   Incomplete applications will not be considered. BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/Assistant-Director--Law-Career-Planning---Development_R0007334": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Law Career Planning & Development",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted 18 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY Willamette University School of Law is the oldest law school in the Pacific Northwest and a continued leader in Oregon and the Northwest in employment outcomes for its graduates.  The Office of Career Planning and Development is dedicated to advancing our students’ career goals through a diverse array of professional programs designed to facilitate the transition from law student to attorney. We are proud to provide robust support for mentoring relationships with attorneys and judges, connections to attorneys in different practice areas, networking opportunities, career development seminars and informational panels. Our goal is to prepare students for a successful career search and career. The Assistant Director for Career Planning & Development works in partnership with and supports the Assistant Dean and Associate Director of Career Planning & Development in providing career counseling, job and career training, and networking opportunities for students and alumni. Responsibilities include, but are not limited to, assisting in developing programs and workshops, managing relationships with the legal community, collecting employment data, and assisting with the Externship Program. Service to law students and alumni includes, among others, the following activities: Providing guidance and counseling to students and alumni Developing and conducting a variety of workshops and programs Providing networking opportunities Facilitating interview and job fair programs Collecting employment data  Participating in local and state bar and/or other professional activities Cooperation with University career services office to advance institutional career services objectives RESPONSIBILITIES Direct Services to Students and Alumni Provide one-on-one or group career counseling sessions, in person or virtually. Assist with planning and presentation of workshops, panels, and training events. Identif y and communicate career opportunities to students and alumni. Strategize with students and alumni on career goals and steps to achieve them. In cooperation with Assistant Dean and Associate Director(s), c ollaborate with faculty and staff on programming. Assist with implementation of various career programs such as the pro bono honors program, public interest law program, on campus interviewing (OCI), mock interviews, judicial clerkships, and/or the attorney-mentor program. Guide students who are exploring various routes to bar licensure, including the bar exam in various states, as well as new paths such as Oregon ’s Supervised Practice Portfolio Examination. Assist with the Externship Program by developing externship opportunities, supporting hosts, supervisors, and students, and coordinating site visits to ensure alignment with educational objectives. Reporting & Statistic Surveys Assist with collection of data regarding employment and externship for strategic planning and external reporting to entities such as the ABA, U.S. News, and NALP. Develop one-on-one relationships with students and recent graduates to provide guidance and job opportunities, and to facilitate data collection.  Outreach to Students, Employers, Alumni/Marketing/Public Relations Develop and maintain relationships with employers, attorneys and judges across the legal spectrum. Where assigned by Associate Director or Assistant Dean, p articipate in local and state bar or other professional activities , including the Oregon Legal Recruiters Association. Represent Willamette Law at professional events, accompanying students where needed. Communicate job and networking opportunities to students and alumni via various media channels. Assist with Externship program and Externship class coordination, enrollment, and implementation. Additional Administrative Duties Work with communications staff on messaging. Stay informed on legal job market trends. Develop familiarity with appropriate software used for reporting, tracking, and communications. Provide web-based resources for students and alumni, including online job listings. Other duties or projects as may be assigned by Assistant Dean or Dean. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Degree: Juris Doctor from an ABA accredited law school but Masters Degree or other substantial relevant experience may be considered as well. Experience: One or more years of r elevant professional experience , with a preference for applicants with legal or other relevant job experience. Strong communication, problem-solving, design-thinking, and organizational skills. Commitment to legal education and cultural diversity: Ability to work effectively with people regardless of their age, gender, gender orientation, ethnicity, or religion. Technical Skills: comfortable with social media tools, Google Workspace Suite and Microsoft Office systems, with the ability to learn new software systems quickly . Ability to work collaboratively and professionally with various stakeholders to establish and maintain harmonious and productive professional relationships. Preferred Understanding of the evolving legal employment landscape, especially in Oregon. Demonstrated success in enhancing and expanding professional opportunities and skills for students and alumni. Typical Work Schedule: Monday through Friday, some evening and weekend work; occasional travel may also be required in connection with employer visits. Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Typical work is performed indoors in a normal office environment   Salary: $65,000 annually You will need to upload the following documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing the required/desired qualifications and presenting any other applicant characteristics which deserve emphasis Current resume   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received until the position is filled.   Incomplete applications will not be considered.  BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/The-John-Olbrantz-Curator-of-Collections-and-Exhibitions_R0007370": {
      "title": "The John Olbrantz Curator of Collections and Exhibitions",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted 19 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY The Hallie Ford Museum of Art (HFMA) at Willamette University supports student learning at the university as well as faculty and student research, while also serving as a vital cultural resource for campus, the greater Salem community, and the entire Northwest region. Founded in 1998, HFMA has a primary programming focus on art from the Pacific Northwest, with significant holdings of modern and contemporary Northwest art, customary and historic Native American basketry and regalia, and twentieth century craft. In addition, the museum’s survey collection includes objects from the ancient Mediterranean, Asia, Europe, Africa, Oceania, and the Americas. The museum presents an ambitious program of exhibitions with three changing exhibition galleries and three galleries dedicated to the permanent collection – in a total of 10,000 square feet of gallery space across two floors.   This endowed Curator of Collections and Exhibitions position works closely with the Maribeth Collins Director toward the care of collections and development of exhibitions at the museum. The position will care for and manage the permanent collection (approximately 13,000 objects), develop temporary exhibitions, and work with other museum staff on the curatorial efforts related to the permanent collection galleries. The position manages all incoming and outgoing shipping. Additionally, this position represents the museum on and off campus, developing relationships with the various communities that we serve to help guide decisions around collections and exhibitions at HFMA. The Curator of Collections and Exhibitions will contribute to and support HFMA’s strategic plan, institutional initiatives, and commitment to equitable internal and public practices. This position reports to the Maribeth Collins Director of the Hallie Ford Museum of Art. The successful candidate will work independently under applicable University policies, procedures, regulations, and guidelines. Additionally, this position supervises and works closely with a part-time registrar, as well as work-study students and interns from Willamette University. RESPONSIBILITIES Manages and provides proper care, storage, preservation of the permanent collection; with the Registrar will respond to incoming offers of donations from collectors, artists, and galleries; chairs Collection Committee meetings where donation offers are discussed and reviewed; oversees the cataloging, accessioning, photographing, and storage of objects in the collection. Oversees the creation and maintenance of the Hallie Ford Museum of Art’s collection records and collection policies, including the collection database, digital images, and hard files related to the objects in the permanent collection; ensures the implementation of the collection policy; conducts research on objects in the permanent collection as needed. Oversees the packing, unpacking, and condition reports for incoming and outgoing loans to/from the Hallie Ford Museum of Art. Responsible for the planning and coordination of shipping arrangements for incoming and outgoing loans and acquisitions to/from the Hallie Ford Museum of Art. Identifies and prioritizes collection management projects in consultation with the Director; identifies potential funding sources for collection management projects; may write collection-related grants; serve as project and budget manager for grant-funded collection management projects. Provide direction and context for conservation of objects in the permanent collection; oversees the work of contract conservators. Coordinates with the Office of University Services about fine arts insurance. Reviews and makes recommendations to the Director about outgoing loan requests and the ongoing rotation of objects in the permanent collection galleries; responds to inquiries from campus regarding campus art loans; reviews requests and assists in the selection of artworks for campus loans; oversees the installation and maintenance of art loans on campus as well as the Salem Convention Center. This position provides logistical and project management support to all exhibitions; makes recommendations to the Director about temporary exhibitions drawn from the permanent collection or external sources; may coordinate and facilitate all aspects of the development of an exhibition – including curatorial concept, curatorial decisions, securing loans, writing didactic texts, and arranging shipping; Provides support to student and faculty exhibition projects. This position will periodically need to assist with preparatory work (i.e., matting, framing, hanging artwork). Along with Curator of Education, serves as a liaison with the faculty in the planning for class visits to the museum as well as integrating its permanent collection and temporary exhibitions into the liberal arts curriculum of the college; provides faculty, staff, students, and researchers with hands-on access and virtual access to the permanent collection as well as accompanying information about the objects in the collection. Advises the Exhibition Designer/Chief Preparator on security, lighting, and display of objects in permanent and temporary exhibitions. Performs related duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum of five to seven years of progressively responsible curatorial experience in an art museum or gallery. Knowledge of American Alliance of Museums (AAM) best practices and standards. Ability to establish and maintain harmonious relations with collectors, donors, faculty, staff, students, members, visitors, and others. Ability to execute those physical activities required to perform the essential functions of the job. Strong research and writing skills. Excellent interpersonal and communications skills. Project management skills and ability to manage multiple projects at the same time. Advanced degree in art, art history, museology, or related field required. Any combination of additional education, training, or experience that provides the knowledge, skills, and abilities required to perform the essential functions of the position. Typical Work Schedule: Monday through Friday, 8 am to 5 pm. Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Typical work requires moving artwork, equipment, supplies, furnishings, and exhibition-related materials. Frequently and safely lift up to 25 pounds and occasionally lift between 26 and 50 pounds.   Salary: $70,000 annually You will need to upload the following documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing the required/desired qualifications Current resume   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received until the position is filled.   Incomplete applications will not be considered.  BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Portland-Campus/Campus-Safety-Officer_R0007367": {
      "title": "Campus Safety Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted 21 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY Campus Safety Officers are responsible for helping ensure the safety and security of Willamette University's students, employees, visitors, and property during their assigned shifts. They deal with all violations of law and campus policy in an appropriate and equitable manner and positively engage with diverse individuals and groups in support of an inclusive environment. The Campus Safety Officer position reports to the Associate Director of Campus Safety. This position is responsible for responding to situations utilizing a set of well-defined policies, procedures, and expectations. Must be able to perform duties with independence. Provides direction and supervision to work-study students but has no formal responsibility for supervision. Public relations are an important part of this position. Requires strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills to exchange information with individuals both inside and outside the University. Requires an understanding of a culturally diverse community and the need to interact in a manner that fosters positive relationships in respectful, equitable, and inclusive ways.   Duties and Responsibilities Campus Safety Officers are responsible for helping ensure the safety and security of Willamette University's students, employees, visitors, and property during their assigned shifts. Patrol university grounds and facilities to ensure a safe and secure environment, including on foot and in vehicles, patrol campus facilities, parking lots, grounds, and the interior of all buildings on campus. Monitor all activity and confront any suspicious behavior on Campus. Escort visitors and intruders off campus if required. Issue citations and warnings to violators. Interview individuals involved in incidents, gather information and write reports on crimes, violence, vandalism, fires, and other illegal activities on campus. Give accounts of people involved, who are affected, and what measures were taken to minimize or eliminate the incidents. Respond quickly to all calls to Campus Safety for assistance and contact local Police, Fire, or Medical personnel when necessary. Provides medical care at the basic first aid/CPR/AED level as provided by the American Red Cross or its equivalent. Coordinates with medical responders when a higher level of care is required. Responds to notifications from the security and fire alarms across campus that are monitored by campus Safety. Contribute to the support and implementation of the University Strategic Plan. Adhere to University policies and procedures. Updates daily activity logs as needed and in a timely manner; completes written incident and investigative reports in a timely, clear, and well-written manner. Perform other duties as assigned.   Minimum Qualifications Must meet minimum qualifications for certification standards set by the Department of Public Safety Standards and Training (DPSST). DPSST requires candidates to be a minimum of 18 years of age to obtain an Unarmed Private Security certification. High school diploma or GED Minimum of two years experience in a security position or two years of college-level law enforcement or criminal justice courses And/ Or any combination of education and experience that provides the required knowledge, skills, and abilities to perform the position's essential functions. Ability to operate a computer, with demonstrated skills in Outlook, Word, Excel, and other security-related programs.  Valid driver’s license within 60 days of hire. CPR/AED Certification or obtain within 90 days of employment. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills are necessary, as well as the ability to actively communicate with various constituents, including faculty, staff, administration, and the general public. Demonstrated ability to efficiently and effectively solve problems and initiate and implement projects independently. Ability to multi-task with accuracy and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Experience working with diverse populations. Preferred Qualifications Customer service experience. Experience working in a security-related field. Experience working at a college or university. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Typical schedule requires 40 hour per work with possible required overtime as needed Requires standing for long periods of time and taking many stairs/runs as part of your regular patrol duties.  Requires to kneel/crawl, twist, lift, carry, push, and pull objects that weigh up to 50 pounds. Salary: $23.93/ hour   You will need to upload the following document as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Current Resume   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received until the position is filled.   Incomplete applications will not be considered.  BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/Facilities-Services-Technician-I_R0007316": {
      "title": "Facilities Services Technician I",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY The Facilities Department at Willamette University is responsible for the daily operations, maintenance and upkeep of approximately 1.4 million square feet of academic, residential, administrative and off campus properties situated on approximately 60 acres with all the associated utilities and infrastructure. The Facilities Services Technician I performs a variety of skilled and semi-skilled custodial/ housekeeping duties in University buildings as assigned by Facilities Services Leadership. Candidate will be performing assigned duties within a team oriented work environment to effectively and efficiently provide custodial/ housekeeping services and to maintain the high cleanliness standards of Willamette University. Candidate must be service oriented and be able to work harmoniously with other staff, faculty, students and visitors on Campus while performing the duties of this position.  Assignments as to type of work and location of work duties may vary depending upon the work required to be performed on any given day. Candidate must be flexible to work whatever shift the university assigns. RESPONSIBILITIES Perform job duties in an efficient and professional manner within given deadlines to assure the cleanliness and maintenance of all areas assigned. Work independently, as well as, work harmoniously with all staff in a team oriented work environment to maintain the high cleanliness standards of the University. Typical job responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following daily tasks: Dust mop, sweep, wet mop, and / or spray buff hard surface floors as required. Scrub, strip, seal, wax and refinish floors using machines on a regular basis as directed. Vacuum all rugs, carpets, furniture and drapes. Dust and wipe down/clean walls, doors, windows, waste receptacles, light fixtures, vents, radiators, and other above-floor surfaces. Dust, clean, and polish furniture. Clean and sanitize all restroom surfaces and fixtures. Replenish supplies daily. Clean all blackboards / whiteboards, erasers, and chalk troughs and restock chalk. Clean classrooms and offices. Rearrange classroom furniture to standard set ups. Clean halls, stairways, elevators, lobbies, building entrances and exterior porches. Replace accessible light bulbs immediately when out. Notify supervisor on inaccessible bulbs. Shampoo carpet using extraction, foam and/or dry cleaning methods. Move furniture as required or as requested by supervisor. Unplug sinks, toilets and drains when required. Assists with laundry tasks and distribution of laundry to assigned areas. Provides coverage for other staff members that are out due to illness, vacation, or other issues. Notify supervisor on duty in a timely manner of any equipment or facilities needing repair or replacement. Responsible for practicing safe work habits, utilizing safety equipment/PPE’s as required and immediately reporting unsafe conditions to a supervisor. To understand and employ only those custodial methods and cleaning products as approved by the University to preserve the University’s facilities. Be able to calmly and effectively assist in emergency situations. Subject to after hour callbacks for emergencies. Incumbent will carry an on duty phone or radio. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum A minimum of one-year custodial experience is required. Work flexible shifts. Operate mechanical cleaning equipment such as vacuums, buffers, shampooers, water extractors, blowers, wet vacs, high-speed buffers and high-pressure washers. Work independently as well as demonstrate the ability to promote successful community relations with co-workers, the campus community and the public. High school education or GED preferred. Preferred Two to four years related experience preferred. Be aware of safety hazards and have the ability to promote safety as required, including Hazardous Communications rules/regulations, Willamette University’s Safe Working Policy and OR-OSHA regulations. Possess working knowledge of cleaning methods and procedures, materials (such as soap, waxes, and polishes) and the proper care, use, and maintenance of cleaning equipment. Typical Work Schedule: Monday through Friday, 11:30 am to 3:30 pm; p art-time, 20 hours per week. Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Ability to work for extended periods of time while walking, standing, bending, stooping, reaching, and climbing and while exposed to dirt, dust, infectious waste, obnoxious odors, cleaning chemicals, and inclement weather in areas sometimes dimly lit. Requires traveling between campus buildings and routinely traversing stairs and uneven surfaces. Frequently and safely lift up to 25 pounds and occasionally lift between 26 and 50 pounds. Position requires moving equipment, supplies, furnishings, and event-related materials. May require occasional schedule flexibility to support campus events and operational needs. An employer paid pre-employment physical is required. Salary: $19.13/hour You will need to upload the following two documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing qualifications Current resume Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. ​Incomplete applications will not be considered. BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/Instructional-Technology-Associate_R0007141": {
      "title": "Instructional Technology Associate",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY The Instructional Technology Associate is a collaborative role within Willamette University Information Technology (WITS) division, and is responsible for supporting and advancing the University’s Learning Management System and broader instructional technology environment. This position partners closely with stakeholders to promote accessibility and best practices of academic technologies across online and hybrid learning environments. This role serves as a key resource for LMS support, and a collaborative resource for emerging educational technologies including responsible applications of artificial intelligence in teaching and learning. The position works closely with Academic Technology and campus partners to enhance the overall digital learning experience for students and faculty. RESPONSIBILITIES Learning Management System Support: Serve as a technical resource for the University’s learning   management system (Canvas), supporting course delivery and effective use of LMS features and   integrations. LMS End User Support: Serve as primary triage and escalation for all support requests related to   learning management and related systems. Academic Technology Support: Support instructional technologies used in course delivery and academic   engagement (including AI-enabled tools and LTI’s in our Learning Management System). Collaboration and Coordination: Collaborate with Academic Technology, WITS teams, faculty and   campus stakeholders to support optimization of the Learning Management System. Accessibility and Usability: Support accessibility best practices and promote inclusive design principles   within digital learning environments. Assist with projects as delegated related to documentation and support resources to strengthen   institutional use of instructional technologies. Additional Duties: Perform other related duties as assigned in support of evolving academic technology   and instructional needs. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum : Bachelor’s degree preferred in a related field, or equivalent professional experience. Preferred Experience supporting learning management systems such as Canvas or similar platforms. Experience supporting instructional technology within higher education or a comparable environment. Excellent communication, collaboration, and relationship-building skills with the ability to translate technical concepts into practical guidance. Strong organizational and project coordination skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities effectively. Understanding of accessibility and usability best practices within digital learning environments. Interest in emerging educational technologies, including responsible applications of artificial intelligence in higher education. Typical Work Schedule: Part-time, 20 hours per week. Schedule is flexible but must provide regular availability between 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m., Monday through Friday, based on departmental needs. ​ Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Ability to perform work in a standard office environment, including prolonged periods of sitting and computer use. Must be able to occasionally move equipment weighing up to 25 pounds and travel between campus locations as needed. Salary: $40-45/hour, depending on experience You will need to upload the following two documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing qualifications Current resume Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. ​Incomplete applications will not be considered. BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Portland-Campus/PNCA-Dean-of-Students-for-Community-Care---Inclusion_R0007285": {
      "title": "PNCA Dean of Students for Community Care & Inclusion",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY Reporting to the Vice President of Student Affairs at Willamette University in Salem, the Dean of Students for Community Care and Inclusion serves as the senior student affairs administrator  and primary manager for the Office of Student Affairs at the Pacific Northwest College of the Arts (PNCA) in Portland, with approximately 400 undergraduate and 100 graduate students, with 100 primarily first-year students reside in apartment-style living in “ArtHouse”.The Dean collaborates with campus partners across the College and the larger University community to create environments and initiatives that support students’ sense of belonging, wellbeing, and engagement that fosters students’ success. The Dean serves as the supervisor for 2 professional staff members: the Assistant Director of Student Affairs who also serves as the live-in professional for ArtHouse, and the Director of Community Care and Health Education. The Dean oversees the advocacy and support efforts for students experiencing distress through the student CARE process that includes a multidisciplinary team that works to assist students in need of personal care and/or academic resources. The Dean also oversees the student code of conduct at PNCA which ensures the fair and equitable management of the student conduct process and student conflict resolution initiatives, and serves as a Deputy Title IX Coordinator. The Dean ensures appropriate oversight for student events, student clubs and organization advising, and student engagement and coordinates new student orientation. The Dean serves in an on-call rotation to support on-call RAs and Campus Safety Officers and for emergency response. The Dean of Students leads efforts to ensure the PNCA Student Affairs department fosters a welcoming campus environment, and aligns with divisional, college, and university policies and goals. The Dean serves as a relationship builder--mentoring, advising, and connecting with students and members of the campus community to identify needs and develop strategies to enhance the student experience at PNCA. The Dean collaborates with the Academic Dean, Campus Security, facilities, faculty, and staff on the PNCA and Salem campuses in accomplishing these goals. RESPONSIBILITIES   General Management Responsibilities Serve as a strategic thought leader who integrates an understanding of student development, wellbeing, cultural fluency, and crisis management in the implementation of initiatives that support student success and thriving communities. Assist in creating and fostering a culture of personal growth and development for Student Affairs staff; provides adequate training and supervision to support supervisees in carrying out their responsibilities and implementing changes to service models, staffing, outreach and other areas in response to evolving student needs. Provide innovative and strategic leadership for the programmatic and operational functions of the Office of Student Affairs at PNCA. Ensure day-to-day operations include efficient systems to meet professional standards and timely and sensitive response to students', families', and University community members' questions, concerns, and requests for assistance. Work with direct reports to ensure effective and responsible stewardship of finances and resources. Maintain an accessible and visible campus presence; is available for relevant  university events, staff/student-planned community development activities, and emergency response. Management of student conduct and conflict resolution processes Oversee the PNCA student conduct process which includes case distribution, investigations, adjudications, sanctioning and overall management of student conduct. Ensures the student conduct process is administered in a fair, impartial, non-adversarial, restorative, and educational manner and that staff involved in the process are trained appropriately. Ensure staff’s timely follow up to violations of residence hall and University policies. Oversee the direction, training, advising, and facilitation of effective conflict resolution involving students. Coordinate and facilitate dispute resolution processes as needed. Serve as an Administrative/University Hearing Officer, investigator, or member of the University conduct panel hearing board for high level judicial and Title IX cases as needed. Lead efforts to educate the University community on policies, procedures, and/or resources pertaining to student conduct/behavioral expectations and supporting students in distress. Collaborate with the Academic Dean and faculty on student behavior concerns. ​Management of student care and case management processes Serve as the Chairperson for the CARE Team. Work closely with the Director of Community Care and Health Education to prepare meeting agendas, and ensure team input, case assignments and timely follow up. Work with the AVP/Dean of Students and Assistant Dean for Community Care & Inclusion in Salem to establish CARE Team protocols and training to CARE team members and community as needed. Monitor communication/outreach efforts to students. Oversee PNCA crisis management response and protocols for supporting students in distress; work collaboratively with PNCA faculty and staff and Salem campus staff as needed. In collaboration with the Associate Dean for Health and Wellbeing, oversees post- hospitalization support to students returning from a mental/behavioral health setting and transitioning back to the University environment. New Student and Family Orientation Lead the development and coordination of the new student and family orientation program in collaboration with direct reports, the Academic Dean, and appropriate faculty. Create the new student and family orientation communication plan (e.g., website, e-mails, social media) with campus partners. Coordinate opening logistics with campus partners. Student Engagement Establish goals and priorities for student engagement. Ensure programs/initiatives promote students’ sense of belonging, community development, and overall well-being. Ensure the on-going development of initiatives in partnership with faculty and staff based on community interests and student needs. Ensure the development of the Student Council and solicit feedback from members of the student council on the creation of policies, initiatives, and programs. Meet regularly with student leaders. Ensure a clear club registration process and orientation and training for student leaders. Ensure fiscal guidance and ethical use for student activities fund usage and expenditures. Maintains visibility through on-campus presence, attending events and engaging in appropriate outreach to develop and maintain relationships with students, faculty, and staff. Divisional Leadership Attend divisional meetings and trainings. Chaperone and assist in organization of large, campus-wide programs as needed such as orientation events, accepted students days, First Thursdays and commencement. Assist in leading task forces, presenting training topics, and executing divisional and university projects as needed. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum A Master's degree in Student Personnel, Counseling, or related discipline is required Strong writing and presentation skills Ability to implement problem solving strategies to manage everyday challenges such as crisis response, conflict management, community development, student activism, and student/family concerns in a fast-paced environment. Can function competently under pressure and effectively diffuses crisis situations to demonstrate sound judgment Supervision experience Experience in higher education or student affairs ​ Preferred Preferred candidates have at least 10 years of progressive student affairs experience in crisis management, student care, advising, supervision, student conduct, and program planning. Previous experience at a college of art is preferred. Possess a sound background in student/human development theory, Title IX, student mental health, crisis management, supervision, community development and student success. Knowledge of relevant laws such as Title IX, Clery Act, Campus Sexual Violence prevention (Campus SaVE) Act, Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), the ADA Involvement in local, regional, and national organizations supporting the field of student affairs (such as ACPA and NASPA). Typical Work Schedule: In-person work 8 am to 5 pm, remote work only when students are not in session; participate in on-call rotation Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Typical work is performed indoors in a normal office environment. Salary: $100,000-115,000 Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received until position is filled. You will need to upload the following two documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing the required/desired qualifications and presenting any other applicant characteristics which deserve emphasis Current Resume ​ Incomplete applications will not be considered. BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Portland-Campus/Assistant-Director-of-Student-Affairs--PNCA_R0007233": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Student Affairs, PNCA",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY Reporting to the Dean of Students for Community Care & Inclusion, the Assistant Director of Student Affairs assists in overseeing the co-curricular engagement of 400 undergraduate students and 40 graduate students. In collaboration with the PNCA Student Affairs team, the Assistant Director’s responsibilities are aimed at creating inclusive environments, events, and initiatives that support students’ sense of belonging, holistic learning, well-being, leadership development, and engagement that fosters students’ success. In close coordination with the Residential Life and Housing team on Willamette University’s Salem Campus, the Assistant Director assumes responsibility for the operation of ArtHouse, an apartment-style residential facility in downtown Portland that houses 130 first-year students and includes the supervision of 4-6 Resident Assistants. This is a live-in position and requires some evening and weekend availability. The Assistant Director serves as a University conduct officer and is a member of the Student Care Team, ensuring case management support and assistance to students in crisis and distress and serving in an on-call rotation for emergency and response. RESPONSIBILITIES Engagement & Community Development Advise and support recognized undergraduate and graduate student organizations and Arthouse Hall Council; meet regularly with their executive teams. Oversee student leaders in planning and implementing events. Lead and/or assist in the program planning and implementation of campus-wide events and initiatives such as New Student and Family Orientation, Family Weekend, Holiday Art Fair, Cultural Heritage Months, other affinity-based programming, personal development opportunities, and social events & programs. Develop community-building and educational initiatives based on the Student Affairs Division curriculum and PNCA goals that support student learning, engagement, and belonging. Develop and support student recognition initiatives and promote leadership opportunities. Allocate and maintain student lockers. Maintain visibility, engage regularly with students, and attend campus and community programs. Provide budgetary oversight to assigned budget accounts. Ensure compliance with institutional budget policies. Arthouse Facilities Operations & Residence Life Assist in overall administration of the residence life and housing program. Coordinate facility management, housing operations, and communication with residents with PTLA property management group. Assist in the development and evaluation of housing policies, procedures, and goals. Educate RAs and residents on safety and security concerns and appropriate emergency response procedures. Assumes other duties as assigned by the Director of Residential Life and Housing and Dean of Students for Community Care & Inclusion Recruit, train, and supervise 4-6 Resident Advisors and Office of Student Affairs student employees. Provide ongoing feedback and support to student staff. Oversees RA responsibilities in the communication of community expectations of students and response to behavioral issues. Establish a sense of teamwork and collaboration among RAs. Provide team-building opportunities to promote good RA relations. ​Student CARE, Conduct, and Conflict Resolution Serve as a Conduct Hearing officer.  Investigate incidents, facilitate Administrative Hearings, determine responsibility, assign sanctions as appropriate, make appropriate referrals, and write related conduct letters. Mediate conflicts between students, facilitating discussions to resolve disputes and promote an inclusive and supportive living environment. Support students in distress and help facilitate student wellbeing Serve as a member of the CARE team, responding to CARE Reports as a case manager to provide support to students experiencing difficulties. Crisis Response and Management Serve in a scheduled on-call rotation to respond to student emergencies and crises, providing immediate support and intervention. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree Two years experience working in residence life on a university campus. Demonstrated experience organizing events and programs. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Demonstrated experience and comfortability with providing support to students in distress, mental health challenges, and interpersonal conflict Evidence of cultural sensitivity and competence for effectively engaging and working within a culturally diverse community Preferred Qualifications Masters Degree in college student personnel, higher education leadership, social work, psychology or related field. Experience coordinating and/or organizing projects and programs in a diverse environment within higher education, preferably experience working with art students Experience responding to and working with diverse, at-risk, disadvantaged, and historically underrepresented student populations. Demonstrated experience implementing best practices and strategies in student engagement, development, persistence retention, campus services, leadership development, and student success. Experience working at an art and design college Excellent multi-project organizational management skills. Experience providing support to students experiencing academic challenges, mental health concerns, roommate conflicts, and personal concerns. Experience responding to crisis and/or early intervention (i.e., on-call for emergency response, conflict mediation, responding to medical or mental health emergencies, fire alarms, etc.). Typical Work Schedule Monday through Friday, 8 am to 5 pm Evening and weekend hours as needed and are common This position also serves on a scheduled on-call rotation Physical Demands & Working Conditions: Typical work is performed indoors in a normal office environment. Salary: $54,654 annually You will need to upload the following documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing the required/desired qualifications and presenting any other applicant characteristics which deserve emphasis Current resume   Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received until the position is filled.   Incomplete applications will not be considered.  BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    },
    "https://willamette.wd501.myworkdayjobs.com/WillametteUniversityJobs/job/Salem-Campus/Facilities-Management-Purchaser_R0007089": {
      "title": "Facilities Management Purchaser",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-05-26\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nABOUT WILLAMETTE UNIVERSITY: Willamette University , founded in 1842, is a nationally-renowned private university enrolling 2800 students across five colleges. Our historic campus, adjacent to the Oregon State Capitol, hosts a distinguished undergraduate liberal arts college in Salem, Oregon. Our downtown Portland campus is home to the Pacific Northwest College of Art (PNCA). We offer a wide range of professional graduate programs, including Oregon’s top-ranked MBA program , the Northwest’s oldest law school, as well as MA/MFA programs at PNCA and master’s degrees in data science and computer science. Willamette University provides a high-value and comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees. Our Benefits Summary includes options for health, dental, life insurance, retirement, substantial paid time off, tuition and more. If you need assistance applying for a job or have any questions on recruitment at Willamette, please contact our HR Department , hr@willamette.edu or call us at 503-370-6210. POSITION SUMMARY The Facilities Department at Willamette University is responsible for the daily operations, maintenance and upkeep of approximately 1.4 million square feet of academic, residential, administrative and off campus properties situated on approximately 60 acres with all the associated utilities and infrastructure. The Facilities Management Purchaser provides centralized purchasing in support of all Facilities departments. Daily duties will primarily involve the procurement, purchasing and issuing of parts and supplies for the Facilities Maintenance team. This position also receives and distributes freight deliveries, issues and reconciles P numbers, reconciles P-Card purchase records, prices out and completes work orders. Updates and maintains all Facilities SDS and equipment warranty records. Serves as backup to Facilities office staff as required. RESPONSIBILITIES Manages and encourages Facilities Management workplace expectations, valuing all persons and treating everyone with dignity and respect. Interacts in a professional manner with suppliers on a day to day basis. Responsible for sourcing and purchasing parts and materials for all Facilities departments at the best value to the University. Procures and arranges for the pickup or delivery of all parts, materials, and supplies used by Facilities departments on a daily, routine or ongoing basis. Procures and arranges for the pick up or delivery of parts, materials, furnishings and supplies for routine, special or emergency projects. Verifies and tracks received inventory and completes inventory reports and logs. Reconciles shipping invoices and receiving reports to ensure count accuracy. Receives, unloads, and processes deliveries. Refuses acceptance of damaged, unacceptable, or incorrect items. Troubleshoots vendor delivery issues and oversees the return process. Maintains accurate purchasing records. Issues P numbers and reconciles the P number log. Maintains records and files for effective, accurate tracking of maintenance and repairs, ensuring proper coding and monitoring of all cost and quality factors. Responsible for updating prices and materials in the Asset Planner Service Request system. Maintains price lists and price agreements. May write bids and specifications as needed. Is responsible for acquiring and keeping updated records of all SDS documents that pertain to Facilities purchases and for directing the SDS documents to the appropriate departmental personnel. Issues parts and materials as required. Maintains the security of parts and materials under the purchaser’s control. Serves in a backup role for the various Facilities office staff as needed. May supervise a work study student in the picking-up and delivery of parts. If workload permits, performs other Facilities maintenance related duties as required. Subject to after-hours call backs. Performs other duties as assigned. OTHER REQUIREMENTS Accountability: Ability to accept responsibility and account for actions. Customer Oriented: Ability to provide a high level of customer service while following company procedures. Detail Oriented: Ability to pay attention to the minute details of a project or task. Calm & Efficient: Ability to deal with emergencies in a calm and efficient manner. Organized: Possessing the trait of being organized or following a systematic method for tasks. Communication: Ability to communicate positively and effectively with a wide variety of clients in person, by phone, or in writing. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Any combination of education and experience that provides the required knowledge, skills and abilities to perform the essential functions of the position. High school education with some college coursework preferred. Two years of related work experience in building construction, maintenance and custodial/housekeeping or related trades in addition to a minimum of two years’ of experience in purchasing and supplying parts and materials: or equivalent experience in order to successfully perform the duties of the job. Proficient use of a personal computer and software programs such as Word, Excel and any other specialized applications required. Keyboarding, email and maintaining databases required. Willingness and ability to quickly learn new computer skills. Possess a thorough knowledge of building construction, maintenance and custodial/housekeeping related trades and be familiar with Maintenance terminology/operations. Excellent organizational skills and ability to manage competing priorities in order to meet deadlines. Ability to work in a fast paced team oriented environment. Must possess an understanding of accounting processes and mathematics. Must be able to buy selectively through the competitive bidding process. Able to estimate time and materials accurately. Requires considerable contact with individuals outside the University and a high level of contact with individuals within the University, and requires the interpersonal skills necessary for such contact. Experience with record keeping and file maintenance skills to keep accurate records of purchases, purchase orders issued, payments, warranty information, and vendor lists. Must have the ability to effectively interact with a variety of people in a manner that emphasizes strong customer service and portrays the University’s programs, personnel, and philosophies in a positive manner. Knowledge of safety hazards and precautions related to building and equipment maintenance such as OSHA, Hazardous Communications, DEQ, and EPA regulations. Ability to negotiate skillfully in tough situations with both internal and external customers. Have the ability to work independently as well as establish and maintain relations with other staff, faculty and students. Must possess a valid Oregon driver’s license and have a satisfactory driving record in order to drive university vehicles and carts. Preferred Experience in and the ability to become certified in driving a forklift College coursework relevant to the position Typical Work Schedule: 7:30am – 4:00pm M-F Salary: $24.52 per hour (~$51,000 annually) You will need to upload the following two documents as part of your application materials in the \"My Experience\" section labeled Resume: Cover letter addressing qualifications Current resume Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. ​Incomplete applications will not be considered. BACKGROUND CHECK REQUIREMENT: All University positions require that candidates submit to a criminal conviction record check prior to hire. Conviction does not automatically preclude candidates from being hired. Nature of conviction will be considered relative to the duties of the position.   REASONABLE ACCOMMODATIONS STATEMENT: To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. INSTRUCTIONS FOR APPLICANTS: Please read the following carefully before beginning the online application process. To be considered for employment at Willamette University, please complete our online employment application. Your application and all required attachments -- listed at the bottom of the page -- must be submitted for each individual job post that you are interested in applying. Once you submit your application you will not be able to edit your application."
    }
  },
